## Proof

**Setup.** The affine variety $X = V(x^2+y^2-1) \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ is the unit circle, with coordinate ring $\mathbb{R}[X] = \mathbb{R}[x,y]/(x^2+y^2-1)$. The group
$$G = \left\{\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ -b & a\end{pmatrix} : a^2+b^2=1\right\} \cong SO(2)$$
acts on $X$ by $g\cdot(x,y) = (ax+by,\,-bx+ay)$.

**Key observation: the action is transitive.** Take the point $p_0 = (1,0) \in X$. For any $g = \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ -b & a\end{pmatrix} \in G$,
$$g \cdot (1,0) = (a\cdot 1 + b\cdot 0,\; -b\cdot 1 + a\cdot 0) = (a, -b).$$
As $(a,b)$ ranges over all pairs with $a^2+b^2=1$, the point $(a,-b)$ ranges over all of $X$ (since $a^2+(-b)^2 = a^2+b^2 = 1$). Hence
$$\operatorname{Orb}(p_0) = \{(a,-b) : a^2+b^2=1\} = X.$$
So $G$ acts transitively on $X$.

**Invariant polynomials are constant.** Let $f \in \mathbb{R}[X]$ be $G$-invariant, meaning $f(g\cdot p) = f(p)$ for all $g \in G$, $p \in X$. By transitivity, every $p \in X$ lies in the orbit of $p_0 = (1,0)$, so there exists $g \in G$ with $g \cdot p_0 = p$. Then
$$f(p) = f(g \cdot p_0) = f(p_0) = f(1,0).$$
Thus $f$ takes the same value $f(1,0)$ at every point of $X$, i.e., $f$ is constant on $X$.

**Constants are indeed invariant.** Every constant polynomial $c \in \mathbb{R}$ trivially satisfies $c = g \cdot c$ for all $g$.

**Conclusion.** The set of all $G$-invariant polynomials on $X$ is precisely the ring of constants:
$$\mathbb{R}[X]^G = \mathbb{R}.$$

